/* Sec+ Gym Cards — part d45 */
export const CARDS_D45 = [
 /* ===== harden (second half) ===== */
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Host-based firewall — what it controls",a:"#Traffic in/out of one machine\nPer-host packet filter — blocks ports and apps on that endpoint only",s:"Filters traffic on a single endpoint",
  x:"A host-based firewall runs on the endpoint itself (Windows Defender Firewall, iptables/nftables) and permits or blocks inbound and outbound connections for that one machine. It complements, not replaces, the network firewall because it still protects a laptop on hotel Wi-Fi. Exam cue: 'block a specific application from communicating on a workstation' or 'protect a mobile host off the corporate network' points to the host-based firewall."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"HIPS vs HIDS — the difference",a:"#HIPS can block; HIDS only alerts\nBoth watch one host's files, processes and calls — prevention acts inline",s:"Host agent that blocks, not just alerts",
  x:"A host-based intrusion detection system monitors a single machine for suspicious activity and raises alerts, while a host-based intrusion prevention system sits inline and can stop the action, such as killing a process or blocking a system call. The exam distinguishes them by the verb: 'detect and notify' is HIDS, 'prevent or stop' is HIPS. Contrast with network-based NIDS/NIPS, which watch traffic segments rather than one host."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"harden",q:"EDR vs XDR — what the X adds",a:"#XDR correlates beyond the endpoint\nEDR watches endpoints; XDR fuses endpoint, network, email, cloud, identity telemetry",s:"Correlates endpoint, network, cloud and email data",
  x:"Endpoint detection and response agents record process, file and network behavior on hosts, hunt for indicators, and let analysts isolate or remediate a machine. Extended detection and response widens that view by ingesting and correlating data from firewalls, email gateways, cloud workloads and identity systems in a single console. On the exam, 'endpoint only' versus 'cross-domain correlation with a unified view' is the distinguishing phrase."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Disabling unused ports and protocols — the hardening goal",a:"#Shrink the attack surface\nEvery listening service is a potential entry point — turn off what isn't needed",s:"Reduce attack surface by closing unneeded services",
  x:"Every open port represents a listening service that could be exploited, so hardening means stopping and disabling services that the system's role does not require, such as Telnet, SMBv1 or an unused web server. Auditing with a port scanner or netstat/ss confirms what is actually listening. Exam cue: 'reduce the attack surface' or 'unnecessary services running' signals disabling ports and protocols."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Why default passwords must be changed at deployment",a:"#Defaults are public knowledge\nVendor credentials are published online and scanned for by bots",s:"Vendor credentials are published and scanned for",
  x:"Routers, IoT devices, printers and appliances ship with well-known default credentials that are listed in public databases and tried automatically by botnets such as Mirai. Changing them during initial configuration is one of the first hardening steps, alongside disabling unused accounts. On the exam, a device compromised 'immediately after install' or 'admin/admin' points to default credentials as the root cause."},
 {obj:"4.1",c:"harden",q:"Removing unnecessary software — why it matters",a:"#Fewer packages, fewer patches\nUnused applications still carry vulnerabilities you must maintain",s:"Unused apps still add vulnerabilities to maintain",
  x:"Bloatware, trial software and unused utilities each bring their own vulnerabilities, update cycles and potential misconfigurations, so hardening removes anything the system's function does not require. A minimal installation is easier to keep patched and to monitor. The exam pairs this with disabling services under 'least functionality': if the question stresses 'installed but never used applications', the answer is to remove them."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"harden",q:"Group Policy Objects (GPO) — hardening role",a:"#Centrally enforced Windows settings\nPush password, audit, firewall and lockout rules from Active Directory",s:"Centrally pushes Windows settings from AD",
  x:"Group Policy lets administrators define configuration and security settings once in Active Directory and enforce them on every domain-joined computer and user in scope, covering password policy, lockout, software restrictions, firewall rules and much more. It is the standard way to apply a secure baseline consistently across a Windows fleet. Exam cue: 'apply settings to all domain workstations' or 'prevent users from changing security settings' points to GPO."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"harden",q:"SELinux — what it enforces",a:"#Mandatory access control on Linux\nKernel labels processes and files; policy decides access regardless of owner",s:"Kernel-enforced MAC labels on Linux",
  x:"Security-Enhanced Linux adds mandatory access control to the kernel: every process and object carries a security context label, and a policy determines what each domain may touch even if the file's traditional permissions would allow it. Modes are enforcing, permissive (log only) and disabled. Exam cue: 'confine a compromised daemon on Linux' or 'MAC on a Linux host' signals SELinux (AppArmor is the comparable profile-based tool)."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"harden",q:"File integrity monitoring (FIM) — what it detects",a:"#Unexpected file changes\nHashes system and config files and alerts when they differ from the baseline",s:"Alerts when file hashes differ from baseline",
  x:"File integrity monitoring records a cryptographic hash of critical system binaries, configuration files and registry keys, then periodically or continuously compares current hashes to the baseline and alerts on any change. It catches rootkits, tampered configs and unauthorized patches. On the exam, 'detect modification of system files' or a compliance requirement such as PCI DSS for change detection points to FIM (Tripwire is the classic example)."},
 {obj:"2.5",c:"harden",q:"Application allow list vs deny list",a:"#Allow list: only approved apps run\nDeny list blocks named bad apps; allow list is stricter default-deny",s:"Only explicitly approved executables can run",
  x:"An allow list (whitelist) permits only applications that have been explicitly approved and blocks everything else, which stops unknown malware but requires ongoing maintenance. A deny list (blacklist) blocks specific known-bad programs and lets everything else run, so it is easier to manage but weaker. Exam cue: 'most secure' or 'prevent unapproved software from executing' is allow list; 'block a specific application' is deny list."},

 /* ===== iam ===== */
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Provisioning and de-provisioning — the identity lifecycle",a:"#Create on hire, remove on exit\nAccounts and access granted when needed and revoked promptly when roles end",s:"Grant access on hire, revoke promptly on exit",
  x:"Provisioning creates the account, assigns group memberships and issues credentials when a user joins or changes role; de-provisioning disables or deletes access when they leave. Timely de-provisioning is the control that stops former employees from logging in, and automation via HR-driven workflows makes it reliable. On the exam, 'terminated employee still has access' is a de-provisioning failure; 'new hire needs accounts across systems' is provisioning."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Identity proofing",a:"#Verifying you are who you claim before enrollment\nDocument checks, knowledge questions or in-person validation precede issuing credentials",s:"Verify a real identity before issuing credentials",
  x:"Identity proofing is the process of confirming that an applicant is the real person they claim to be before an account or credential is created, using government ID, biometrics, database checks or an in-person visit. It happens once at enrollment, whereas authentication happens at every login. Exam cue: 'before creating the account' or 'validate the new employee's identity' signals proofing, not MFA."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Federation — the core idea",a:"#Trust another org's identity provider\nUsers authenticate at home; partner systems accept the assertion",s:"Accept logins verified by a partner's IdP",
  x:"Federation links separate security domains so that a user authenticated by their own organization's identity provider can access a partner's or cloud provider's resources without a separate account. It relies on trust relationships and standards such as SAML or OpenID Connect to pass assertions. Exam cue: 'employees of a partner company need access without creating accounts' or 'log in with corporate credentials to a SaaS app' points to federation."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Single sign-on (SSO) — benefit and risk",a:"#One login, many apps\nFewer passwords to manage, but a stolen session unlocks everything",s:"One authentication grants access to many apps",
  x:"Single sign-on lets a user authenticate once and then reach multiple applications without re-entering credentials, which reduces password fatigue and help-desk resets and centralizes control. The trade-off is that a compromised SSO credential or session token gives an attacker the same broad reach, so MFA on the SSO login is essential. Exam cue: 'users complain about too many passwords' or 'authenticate once' signals SSO."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"LDAP — what it is used for",a:"#Querying a directory service\nReads and writes user/group objects; secure with LDAPS on 636",s:"Protocol to query a directory of users and groups",
  x:"The Lightweight Directory Access Protocol is the standard way applications look up and manage objects such as users, groups and computers in a directory like Active Directory. Plain LDAP on TCP 389 sends data in cleartext, so LDAPS on 636 or StartTLS should be used. On the exam, an application that needs to 'look up user attributes' or 'authenticate against the directory' is using LDAP; a cleartext bind is the classic vulnerability."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"OAuth 2.0 — what it grants",a:"#Delegated authorization\nAn app gets a scoped access token to act on your behalf without your password",s:"Delegates limited API access via tokens",
  x:"OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework: a user consents to let a third-party app access specific resources (scopes) on a resource server, and the app receives an access token instead of the user's credentials. It is about what an app may do, not proving who the user is. Exam cue: 'allow an app to read your calendar without giving it your password' is OAuth; when the question is about login identity, look for OpenID Connect."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"SAML — the three roles in the SSO flow",a:"#Principal, identity provider, service provider\nIdP authenticates the user and sends a signed XML assertion to the SP",s:"XML assertion from IdP tells SP the user is authenticated",v:"saml",
  x:"Security Assertion Markup Language is an XML-based standard for exchanging authentication and authorization data. The principal (user) requests a service provider resource, is redirected to the identity provider to authenticate, and returns with a digitally signed assertion the SP trusts. Exam cue: 'XML', 'assertion' or 'enterprise SSO to web applications' signals SAML; the IdP is where the password is actually checked."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"OpenID Connect (OIDC) — relationship to OAuth",a:"#Authentication layer on top of OAuth 2.0\nAdds an ID token (JWT) proving who the user is",s:"Adds identity token on top of OAuth",
  x:"OpenID Connect extends OAuth 2.0 with an identity layer: alongside the access token, the identity provider issues a JSON Web Token called an ID token that asserts who the user is and how they authenticated. It powers 'Sign in with Google' style consumer and mobile logins. On the exam, OAuth alone is authorization; 'OAuth plus login/identity' or 'JWT ID token' means OIDC. SAML is the XML-based enterprise alternative."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Interoperability in identity systems",a:"#Standards so different systems trust each other\nSAML, OIDC, SCIM and LDAP let vendors' IdPs and apps work together",s:"Open standards let mixed vendors share identity",
  x:"Interoperability means identity components from different vendors and organizations can exchange authentication, authorization and provisioning data reliably. It is achieved through open standards such as SAML, OpenID Connect, SCIM for provisioning and LDAP for directory access, and it is a key criterion when choosing an IdP or SaaS app. Exam cue: 'integrate a new cloud app with the existing identity provider' tests whether the candidate picks standards-based options."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Attestation (in IAM and devices)",a:"#Verified proof of a claimed state\nAccess reviews attest entitlements; TPM attests a device booted trusted code",s:"Signed proof that a claim about identity or device is true",
  x:"Attestation is a formal, verifiable statement that something is as claimed. In access management, managers periodically attest that each user's entitlements are still appropriate (access recertification). In device security, a TPM or FIDO authenticator produces a signed attestation that firmware or a key is genuine and unmodified. Exam cue: 'confirm the device is in a trusted state before granting access' or 'periodic review confirming user access is correct' signals attestation."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Mandatory access control (MAC)",a:"#Labels and clearances set by the system\nOwners can't change access — policy compares object label to subject clearance",s:"System-enforced labels; owners can't share",g:"acmodel",
  x:"In mandatory access control the operating system assigns security labels (Top Secret, Secret, Confidential) to objects and clearances to subjects, and access is decided by policy rather than by the data owner. It is the strictest model, used in military and government contexts and implemented on Linux by SELinux. Exam cue: 'classification labels', 'clearance level' or 'users cannot grant access to others' points to MAC."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Discretionary access control (DAC)",a:"#Owner decides who gets access\nEach resource's owner sets its ACL — flexible but easy to over-share",s:"Resource owner sets permissions",g:"acmodel",
  x:"Discretionary access control lets the owner of a file or object grant or revoke permissions to other users at their discretion, which is how standard NTFS and Unix file permissions work. It is flexible but relies on individual judgment, so data can be shared too widely. Exam cue: 'the file creator/owner assigns permissions' or 'every object has an owner who controls the ACL' identifies DAC."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Role-based access control (RBAC)",a:"#Permissions attached to job roles\nUsers inherit rights from roles like 'Accounts Payable', not individually",s:"Rights come from assigned job roles",g:"acmodel",
  x:"Role-based access control groups permissions into roles that mirror job functions, and administrators assign users to roles rather than granting rights one by one. It simplifies provisioning, enforces least privilege consistently and eases audits. Exam cue: 'based on job function', 'new hire in the same department should get the same access' or 'implicit deny with roles' indicates RBAC (do not confuse with rule-based, which uses conditions)."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Attribute-based access control (ABAC)",a:"#Decisions from many attributes\nUser, resource and environment properties evaluated by policy at request time",s:"Policy evaluates user, resource and context attributes",g:"acmodel",
  x:"Attribute-based access control evaluates a policy against attributes of the subject (department, clearance), the object (classification, owner), and the environment (time, location, device health) to decide each request dynamically. It is the most granular model and underpins zero trust policy engines. Exam cue: 'combination of factors such as location, device and data sensitivity' or 'fine-grained, context-aware' signals ABAC."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Rule-based access control",a:"#Fixed rules applied to everyone\nSystem-wide conditions such as firewall ACLs or 'no logins from outside the country'",s:"Global if-then rules independent of identity",g:"acmodel",
  x:"Rule-based access control applies administrator-defined rules uniformly regardless of who the user is, such as a firewall ACL, a rule denying logins outside business hours, or a rule blocking access from certain IP ranges. It is often layered on top of other models. Exam cue: 'rules apply to all users' or 'access is allowed only under specified conditions' points to rule-based; if the rule is specifically about hours, the exam may call it time-of-day restriction."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Time-of-day restrictions",a:"#Access only during set hours\nLogon allowed 8–6 weekdays, denied otherwise — limits off-hours misuse",s:"Logins limited to approved hours",g:"acmodel",
  x:"Time-of-day restrictions specify the hours and days during which an account may authenticate or a resource may be reached, blocking attempts outside that window. They reduce the risk from stolen credentials being used at night and from insiders working when nobody is watching. Exam cue: 'employee should not be able to log in on weekends' or 'restrict access to business hours' maps directly to this control."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Least privilege",a:"#Only the access the task requires\nGrant minimum rights, review regularly, remove what's no longer needed",s:"Minimum rights needed for the job",
  x:"The principle of least privilege states that users, processes and services should hold only the permissions required to perform their function, nothing more. It limits the damage from compromised accounts and malware and reduces the impact of insider misuse. Exam cue: 'administrator uses admin account for daily email', 'service runs as SYSTEM unnecessarily' or 'reduce impact if the account is compromised' all point to enforcing least privilege."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Something you know",a:"#Knowledge factor\nPassword, PIN or passphrase — cheapest, and the easiest to steal or guess",s:"Password, PIN or passphrase",g:"factor",
  x:"The knowledge factor is any secret the user memorizes: passwords, PINs, passphrases and security questions. It is the most common factor and the weakest on its own because it can be phished, guessed, cracked or reused across sites. On the exam, two knowledge items (password plus security question) are still single-factor; true MFA needs a second distinct category."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Something you have",a:"#Possession factor\nSmart card, hardware token, phone app or security key",s:"Smart card, token, phone or security key",g:"factor",
  x:"The possession factor is a physical item the user carries: a smart card, an OTP hardware fob, a FIDO2 security key, or an authenticator app on a phone that generates or receives codes. An attacker must physically obtain or clone the item, which makes it a strong pairing with a password. Exam cue: 'token', 'card' or 'code from a device' indicates something you have; SMS codes count too, though they are the weakest form."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Something you are",a:"#Inherence factor\nFingerprint, face, iris, voice or vein pattern — a biometric",s:"Fingerprint, face, iris or other biometric",g:"factor",
  x:"The inherence factor authenticates using a physical or behavioral characteristic of the person, such as fingerprint, facial geometry, iris pattern, voice or gait. Biometrics are convenient and hard to share, but templates cannot be changed if compromised and accuracy is measured by FAR, FRR and CER. Exam cue: any biometric reader or 'characteristic of the user' is something you are."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Somewhere you are",a:"#Location factor\nGeolocation, IP range or GPS used as an authentication condition",s:"Geolocation or network location check",g:"factor",
  x:"The location attribute uses where the authentication originates, determined by IP address, GPS, or connection to a specific network, as a condition for access. It is usually treated as a supplementary attribute rather than a standalone factor, and underpins impossible-travel detection and geofencing. Exam cue: 'deny logins from outside the country' or 'login from two continents within minutes' points to the location attribute."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Biometric error rates — FAR, FRR and CER",a:"#CER is where FAR equals FRR\nFAR admits impostors (security failure); FRR rejects legitimate users (usability)",s:"Crossover point where false accepts equal false rejects",
  x:"False acceptance rate measures how often a biometric system admits someone it should reject, a security problem; false rejection rate measures how often it turns away a legitimate user, a usability problem. Tuning sensitivity trades one against the other, and the crossover error rate (also called equal error rate) is where they are equal and is used to compare systems. Exam cue: 'lower CER means more accurate'; 'unauthorized user gained access' is FAR."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Hard token vs soft token",a:"#Hard = dedicated device; soft = app\nKey fob or smart card versus an authenticator app on a phone",s:"Physical fob vs authenticator app",
  x:"A hard token is a dedicated physical device such as an OTP key fob, a smart card or a USB security key, which is hard to compromise remotely but can be lost and costs money to issue. A soft token is software, typically a TOTP authenticator app on a smartphone, which is cheaper and convenient but shares the phone's attack surface. Exam cue: 'no additional hardware' points to soft tokens; 'must not depend on the user's phone' points to hard tokens."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"FIDO2 security keys — why they resist phishing",a:"#Public-key challenge bound to the site origin\nThe key signs a challenge only for the legitimate domain, so fake sites get nothing",s:"Origin-bound public-key challenge defeats phishing",
  x:"FIDO2 (WebAuthn plus CTAP) authenticators hold a private key per site and sign a server challenge that includes the web origin, so a look-alike phishing site cannot obtain a valid signature. No shared secret ever leaves the device, unlike OTP codes that a user can be tricked into typing. Exam cue: 'phishing-resistant MFA' or 'hardware security key with a touch' points to FIDO2/WebAuthn."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Password length vs complexity — 701 guidance",a:"#Length matters more than complexity\nLong passphrases beat short symbol-heavy passwords; screen against breached lists",s:"Long passphrases beat short complex strings",
  x:"Modern guidance (NIST SP 800-63B) favors longer passwords or passphrases over forced special-character rules, because length increases entropy against brute force while complexity rules mostly produce predictable substitutions. Checking new passwords against lists of breached or common passwords adds more protection. Exam cue: 'best improvement to password strength' is usually increasing minimum length, not adding symbol requirements."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Password reuse, expiration and minimum age",a:"#History blocks reuse; min age stops cycling\nExpiration forces rotation; minimum age prevents rapid changes back to the old one",s:"History plus minimum age stop users cycling back",
  x:"Password history remembers previous passwords so users cannot reuse them, but without a minimum password age a user can change the password several times in a row to return to the favorite. Expiration (maximum age) forces periodic rotation, though 701-era guidance limits forced rotation to cases of suspected compromise. Exam cue: 'users immediately change back to their old password' is fixed by minimum age; 'same password on multiple sites' is reuse."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Password managers",a:"#Vault of unique generated passwords\nOne strong master credential protects encrypted, per-site secrets",s:"Encrypted vault generating unique per-site passwords",
  x:"A password manager stores credentials in an encrypted vault unlocked by a master password (ideally plus MFA) and generates long, random, unique passwords for each site, which eliminates reuse and supports autofill that resists look-alike domains. The risk concentrates in the master credential and the vault vendor's security. Exam cue: 'users reuse passwords across many sites' with a request for a practical fix points to a password manager."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Passwordless authentication",a:"#No shared secret to steal\nBiometric, security key or device-bound passkey replaces the password",s:"Passkeys or keys replace the password entirely",
  x:"Passwordless authentication removes the password as a factor, relying instead on cryptographic credentials such as FIDO2 passkeys, platform biometrics that unlock a device-bound key, or certificate-based login. Since there is no shared secret, phishing, credential stuffing and password spraying lose their target. Exam cue: 'eliminate credential phishing' or 'users never type a password' signals passwordless; it is a new 701 term."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Just-in-time (JIT) privileged access",a:"#Elevate only for the task window\nAdmin rights granted on request, time-boxed, then automatically removed",s:"Temporary elevation that expires automatically",
  x:"Just-in-time access is a privileged access management practice where administrators hold no standing elevated rights; they request elevation for a specific task, receive it for a limited window and it is revoked automatically. This shrinks the time an attacker could abuse a compromised admin account. Exam cue: 'reduce standing privileges' or 'admins only get rights when they need them' points to JIT."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Password vaulting (PAM)",a:"#Privileged credentials checked out from a vault\nAdmins never learn the real password; the PAM system rotates it after use",s:"PAM system holds and rotates admin passwords",
  x:"Password vaulting stores privileged, shared and service account credentials in a hardened PAM vault; users check out a session or credential with approval and logging, and the vault rotates the password afterwards so it is never reused or known. It addresses shared root and local admin passwords. Exam cue: 'shared administrator passwords are known by many staff' or 'record privileged sessions' points to a PAM vault."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Ephemeral credentials",a:"#Short-lived, single-use secrets\nTokens or certs issued for minutes and discarded — nothing long-lived to steal",s:"Short-lived tokens that expire in minutes",
  x:"Ephemeral credentials are generated on demand for a specific session or task and expire automatically after a short time, so a leaked token or certificate quickly becomes useless. Cloud roles that issue temporary keys and PAM systems that create one-time accounts are common examples. Exam cue: 'avoid long-lived API keys' or 'credentials valid only for the duration of the task' signals ephemeral credentials."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"Kerberos — how it authenticates",a:"#Tickets from a Key Distribution Center\nTGT proves identity; service tickets grant access without resending the password",s:"Ticket-based SSO via KDC on port 88",
  x:"Kerberos is the default authentication protocol in Active Directory: a client authenticates once to the Key Distribution Center, receives a ticket-granting ticket, and then requests time-stamped service tickets for each resource, so the password never crosses the network. It relies on synchronized clocks and uses UDP/TCP 88. Exam cue: 'tickets', 'KDC', 'clock skew causes login failure' or 'Windows domain SSO' indicates Kerberos."},
 {obj:"4.6",c:"iam",q:"RADIUS vs TACACS+ — which for network device administration",a:"#TACACS+ for device admin\nSeparates AAA, encrypts whole payload, TCP 49 — RADIUS is for network access",s:"TACACS+: TCP 49, full encryption, separate AAA",
  x:"RADIUS (UDP 1812/1813) bundles authentication and authorization, encrypts only the password, and is the usual choice for user network access such as VPN and 802.1X. TACACS+ (TCP 49, Cisco-origin) separates authentication, authorization and accounting, encrypts the entire packet, and supports per-command authorization, making it the preferred choice for managing routers and switches. Exam cue: 'granular control over administrator commands on network devices' points to TACACS+."},

 /* ===== ops ===== */
 {obj:"4.2",c:"ops",q:"Asset acquisition and procurement — the security step",a:"#Vet before you buy\nApproved vendors, security requirements in the purchase, supply chain checks",s:"Security requirements set at purchase time",
  x:"Asset management starts at acquisition: the procurement process should require approved suppliers, verify that hardware and software meet security standards, and record the asset before it is deployed. Buying outside the process creates shadow IT that nobody patches or tracks. Exam cue: 'devices purchased by departments without IT involvement' points to a gap in the acquisition/procurement process."},
 {obj:"4.2",c:"ops",q:"Asset assignment and ownership",a:"#Every asset has a named owner\nAn accountable person is responsible for its security, patching and lifecycle",s:"Named person accountable for each asset",
  x:"Assigning ownership records who is accountable for each asset, whether that is the user issued a laptop or the manager responsible for a server. The owner classifies the asset, approves access and answers for its patch status and eventual disposal. Exam cue: 'no one is responsible for updating the system' or 'who approves access to this server' points to the assignment/ownership gap."},
 {obj:"4.2",c:"ops",q:"Asset classification",a:"#Label by sensitivity and criticality\nDrives handling rules, protection level and backup priority",s:"Sensitivity label that dictates protections",
  x:"Classification assigns each asset a category such as public, internal, confidential or restricted based on the impact of its loss or disclosure, and that label determines encryption, access, retention and backup requirements. It must be done before protective controls can be chosen proportionately. Exam cue: 'determine the level of protection required' or 'label data so users know how to handle it' signals classification."},
 {obj:"4.2",c:"ops",q:"Asset inventory and enumeration",a:"#Know what you have\nYou can't patch, monitor or classify a device you haven't discovered",s:"Discover and list every hardware and software asset",
  x:"Inventory is the authoritative record of hardware, software, licenses and data an organization owns; enumeration is the active discovery (network scans, agents, cloud API queries) that finds assets and keeps the inventory accurate. It is the foundation of vulnerability management and CIS Control 1. Exam cue: 'an unknown device was found on the network' or 'first step before a vulnerability scan' points to inventory/enumeration."},
 {obj:"4.2",c:"ops",q:"Sanitization vs destruction vs certification (disposal)",a:"#Wipe to reuse, destroy to end life, certify to prove it\nOverwrite/purge keeps the drive usable; shred/degauss ends it; a certificate documents it",s:"Sanitize for reuse; destroy; certificate proves it",
  x:"Sanitization removes data so media can be reused or resold, using overwriting, cryptographic erase or degaussing (which also ruins magnetic drives). Destruction physically ends the media through shredding, pulverizing or incineration. Certification is the documented proof, often from a third party, that disposal followed policy. Exam cue: 'drive will be reused' means sanitize; 'ensure data can never be recovered' means destroy; 'audit evidence' means certificate of destruction."},
 {obj:"4.2",c:"ops",q:"Data retention in asset management",a:"#Keep only as long as required\nLegal, regulatory and business needs set the period; then dispose securely",s:"Retention period set by law and business need",
  x:"Data retention policy defines how long each type of record must be kept to satisfy legal, regulatory and operational needs, and when it must be destroyed. Keeping data longer than required increases breach exposure and discovery costs; deleting it early can violate law or break a legal hold. Exam cue: 'how long must logs/records be stored' or 'minimize data kept' signals the retention policy."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Vulnerability scanning — what it does and does not do",a:"#Finds known weaknesses, doesn't exploit\nCompares versions and configs to a vulnerability database",s:"Identifies known flaws without exploiting them",
  x:"A vulnerability scan probes hosts and applications, fingerprints software versions and configurations, and compares them against a database of known vulnerabilities and misconfigurations to produce a prioritized report. Unlike a penetration test it does not attempt exploitation, so it is safe to run regularly. Exam cue: 'identify missing patches across all servers' or 'regular automated check' points to vulnerability scanning; 'prove it can be exploited' points to pen testing."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Credentialed vs non-credentialed scan",a:"#Credentialed logs in for deeper results\nSees installed patches and configs; non-credentialed shows the attacker's outside view",s:"Logs in with an account for fewer false positives",g:"scanresult",
  x:"A credentialed scan authenticates to the target and reads patch levels, registry settings and installed software directly, yielding more accurate results with fewer false positives. A non-credentialed scan only probes from the network, like an external attacker would, and infers vulnerabilities from banners and responses. Exam cue: 'most accurate results' or 'reduce false positives' means credentialed; 'view from an outsider's perspective' means non-credentialed."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Static vs dynamic analysis",a:"#Static reads code; dynamic runs it\nSAST finds flaws in source; DAST tests the running application's behavior",s:"Source code review vs testing at runtime",
  x:"Static application security testing analyzes source or compiled code without executing it, catching issues such as hard-coded secrets and unsafe functions early in development. Dynamic testing exercises the running application with inputs, including fuzzing, to find runtime flaws such as injection. Exam cue: 'review the code before compilation' is static; 'send malformed input to the running app' is dynamic; both belong in the SDLC."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Package monitoring",a:"#Watch third-party libraries for new CVEs\nTrack dependencies (SBOM) and alert when a component becomes vulnerable",s:"Tracks dependencies for newly disclosed flaws",
  x:"Package monitoring continuously checks the open-source and third-party components an application depends on against vulnerability feeds and flags when a library needs updating, using tools such as software composition analysis and dependency bots. It addresses supply chain risk like Log4Shell. Exam cue: 'a library used by the application was found vulnerable' or 'software bill of materials' signals package monitoring."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Threat feeds — OSINT, ISACs and dark web",a:"#Sources of threat intelligence\nOSINT is public; ISACs share per sector; dark web reveals leaked data and chatter",s:"Public, sector-sharing and underground intel sources",
  x:"Threat intelligence feeds supply indicators, TTPs and context. Open-source intelligence comes from public sources; Information Sharing and Analysis Centers pool intelligence among members of one industry such as finance or energy; dark web monitoring looks for stolen credentials, leaked data and attacker discussion about the organization. Exam cue: 'industry-specific sharing group' is ISAC; 'company credentials found for sale' is dark web monitoring."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Penetration testing — how it differs from scanning",a:"#Actively exploits to prove impact\nSimulates a real attack under rules of engagement",s:"Actively exploits findings under agreed rules",
  x:"A penetration test goes beyond identifying vulnerabilities to exploiting them, chaining weaknesses and moving laterally to demonstrate real business impact, all within a written scope and rules of engagement. It is point-in-time and more disruptive than scanning, so it is scheduled and authorized carefully. Exam cue: 'demonstrate whether the vulnerability can actually be used' or 'authorized simulated attack' signals penetration testing."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Bug bounty vs responsible disclosure",a:"#Paid program vs coordinated reporting\nBounty rewards outside researchers; disclosure policy tells anyone how to report safely",s:"Rewarded external research vs coordinated reporting",
  x:"A bug bounty program invites external researchers to find and report vulnerabilities in scope for a reward. A responsible (coordinated) disclosure program is the policy and channel through which any finder can report a flaw privately so it is fixed before public release. Exam cue: 'pay researchers for valid findings' is bug bounty; 'researcher notified the vendor and waited 90 days' describes responsible disclosure."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"False positive vs false negative (scan results)",a:"#Positive flags what isn't there; negative misses what is\nFalse negatives are the dangerous ones — a real flaw goes unreported",s:"Reported flaw that isn't real vs missed real flaw",g:"scanresult",
  x:"A false positive is a scanner alert for a vulnerability that does not actually exist, wasting analyst time and eroding trust in the tool. A false negative is a real vulnerability the scanner failed to report, leaving exposure unnoticed. Exam cue: 'the finding was investigated and the patch was already applied' is a false positive; 'the system was breached through a flaw the scan never flagged' is a false negative."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"CVE vs CVSS",a:"#CVE names it; CVSS scores it\nCVE is the identifier; CVSS 0–10 rates severity",s:"Identifier versus 0-10 severity score",
  x:"Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures is a dictionary that gives each publicly known vulnerability a unique ID such as CVE-2021-44228 so everyone refers to the same flaw. The Common Vulnerability Scoring System assigns that flaw a base score from 0 to 10 using metrics like attack vector, complexity and impact. Exam cue: 'which vulnerability to fix first' uses CVSS; 'look up details about a specific published flaw' uses CVE."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Exposure factor (EF) in vulnerability prioritization",a:"#Percent of asset value lost if exploited\nHigher EF plus a critical asset moves the finding up the queue",s:"Share of asset value lost per event",
  x:"Exposure factor expresses the proportion of an asset's value that would be lost if a particular threat is realized, from 0 to 100 percent. In vulnerability management it combines with asset criticality and CVSS to decide what gets fixed first, and it feeds the SLE calculation in quantitative risk analysis. Exam cue: 'what percentage of the asset would be damaged' is EF, not likelihood."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Vulnerability prioritization inputs",a:"#Severity, exploitability, asset value, exposure\nCVSS alone is not enough — is it internet-facing and actively exploited?",s:"Weigh severity, exploitability and asset criticality",
  x:"Prioritizing remediation means ranking findings by CVSS severity, whether a public exploit exists (such as CISA's KEV list), the criticality and classification of the affected asset, its exposure (internet-facing or internal), and environmental factors. A medium flaw on a public payment server can outrank a critical one on an isolated lab box. Exam cue: 'limited time to patch, which first' tests this weighting."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Vulnerability response options",a:"#Patch, segment, compensate, insure, or except\nFix it, isolate it, add a substitute control, transfer cost, or document an approved exception",s:"Patch, isolate, compensating control, insurance or exception",
  x:"Once a vulnerability is confirmed, responses include patching or upgrading, segmenting the vulnerable system away from attackers, applying compensating controls such as a WAF or stricter monitoring, buying cyber insurance to cover residual financial impact, or granting a formal exception with documented risk acceptance when fixing is impossible. Exam cue: 'cannot patch a legacy system' points to segmentation or compensating controls plus an exception."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Validation of remediation",a:"#Rescan, audit, verify\nProve the fix worked before closing the ticket",s:"Rescan and verify the fix actually closed the flaw",
  x:"After remediation, validation confirms the vulnerability is really gone: rescanning the host with the same scanner, auditing configuration or patch records, and verifying manually that the fix did not break function or leave the flaw reachable another way. Skipping validation is how 'patched' systems stay exploitable. Exam cue: 'confirm the patch was successful' or 'next step after applying the fix' is to rescan."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Vulnerability reporting",a:"#Communicate status to stakeholders\nTrends, open critical counts and time-to-remediate for management and auditors",s:"Metrics and trends for management and auditors",
  x:"Reporting turns scan and remediation data into information that stakeholders can act on: number of open findings by severity, mean time to remediate, compliance against SLAs, and trends over time. Technical teams need host-level detail while executives need risk-level summaries and dashboards. Exam cue: 'demonstrate to the board that the program is improving' points to vulnerability reporting metrics."},
 {obj:"4.4",c:"ops",q:"Log aggregation",a:"#Collect logs into one place\nCentral store (syslog/SIEM) so events from many sources can be searched together",s:"Central collection of logs from many sources",
  x:"Log aggregation forwards events from firewalls, servers, endpoints and applications to a central repository, typically via syslog or agents, where they are normalized and stored. Centralizing logs protects them from tampering on a compromised host and enables correlation across systems. Exam cue: 'analysts must log in to each device to review logs' or 'logs were deleted by the attacker on the server' points to the need for aggregation."},
 {obj:"4.4",c:"ops",q:"Alerting and alert tuning",a:"#Reduce noise so real alerts get seen\nAdjust thresholds and rules to cut false positives and analyst fatigue",s:"Adjust thresholds to cut noisy false alarms",
  x:"Alerting notifies analysts when monitored conditions match a rule or threshold. Tuning refines those rules, adjusting thresholds, adding exceptions for known-good activity and suppressing duplicates, so that the alerts that fire are worth investigating. Untuned systems cause alert fatigue and missed incidents. Exam cue: 'analysts are ignoring alerts because most are false positives' calls for alert tuning."},
 {obj:"4.4",c:"ops",q:"Quarantine (monitoring response)",a:"#Isolate the suspect host or file\nCut it off from the network while keeping it for analysis",s:"Isolate suspicious host or file from the network",
  x:"Quarantine is an automated or manual response that isolates a suspicious endpoint, user or file so it cannot spread or communicate while investigators examine it. NAC can move a host to a quarantine VLAN; EDR can network-isolate a machine; antivirus quarantines detected files. Exam cue: 'prevent the infected laptop from reaching other systems without powering it off' is quarantine, which preserves evidence better than shutdown."},
 {obj:"4.4",c:"ops",q:"SCAP",a:"#Standard formats for automated compliance checks\nUses CVE, CCE, CPE, OVAL and XCCDF so tools compare configs consistently",s:"Standardized language for automated config checks",
  x:"The Security Content Automation Protocol is a NIST suite of specifications that lets different scanning tools express vulnerabilities, configurations and benchmarks in the same machine-readable formats, including CVE for flaws, CCE for configuration items, CPE for platform names, OVAL for test logic and XCCDF for checklists. Exam cue: 'automate checking systems against a security baseline using open standards' points to SCAP."},
 {obj:"4.4",c:"ops",q:"Benchmarks (secure configuration)",a:"#Published hardening checklists\nCIS or vendor benchmarks define the expected secure settings to scan against",s:"Published hardening checklist such as CIS",
  x:"A benchmark is a consensus document, such as the CIS Benchmarks or DISA STIGs, listing the recommended secure settings for an operating system, database or cloud service. Configuration scanners compare systems against the benchmark and report deviations. Exam cue: 'industry-standard hardening guide' or 'measure systems against an accepted configuration standard' signals benchmarks; a baseline is the organization's own adopted version."},
 {obj:"4.4",c:"ops",q:"Agent-based vs agentless monitoring",a:"#Agent gives depth; agentless gives reach\nInstalled software reports richly; agentless uses remote protocols with no install",s:"Installed software vs remote protocol collection",
  x:"Agent-based monitoring installs software on each host that collects detailed telemetry, works offline and can take response actions, but it must be deployed and maintained. Agentless monitoring queries systems remotely via SNMP, WMI, SSH or APIs, which is easy to roll out and works for appliances but yields less detail. Exam cue: 'devices where software cannot be installed' points to agentless; 'continuous detailed endpoint visibility' points to agents."},
 {obj:"4.4",c:"ops",q:"SIEM — core function",a:"#Correlate events across sources in real time\nAggregates logs, normalizes, alerts on patterns and supports investigation",s:"Correlates events from many logs to raise alerts",
  x:"A security information and event management platform collects and normalizes logs from across the environment, correlates related events to detect attacks that no single log reveals, and provides dashboards, alerting and retention for compliance. Its value comes from correlation rules and tuning. Exam cue: 'detect an attack that spans the firewall, VPN and domain controller logs' or 'single pane for security events' points to SIEM."},
 {obj:"4.7",c:"ops",q:"SOAR — what it adds to a SIEM",a:"#Automated response playbooks\nOrchestrates tools to enrich, contain and ticket without manual steps",s:"Automates response actions across tools",
  x:"Security orchestration, automation and response takes alerts from a SIEM or other source and executes predefined playbooks: enriching indicators, blocking an IP on the firewall, disabling an account, or opening a ticket, with human approval where needed. It cuts response time and analyst workload. Exam cue: 'automatically isolate the host when the SIEM detects the pattern' or 'reduce repetitive analyst tasks' points to SOAR."},
 {obj:"4.4",c:"ops",q:"SNMP traps",a:"#Device-initiated alerts to a manager\nAgent pushes a notification on events; SNMPv3 adds auth and encryption",s:"Device pushes event notifications to a manager",
  x:"Simple Network Management Protocol normally works by a manager polling agents on port 161, but a trap is an unsolicited message the device sends to the manager on port 162 when something happens, such as a link going down or a threshold being crossed. Only SNMPv3 provides authentication and encryption. Exam cue: 'switch notifies the monitoring server immediately' is a trap; 'community string sent in cleartext' means v1/v2c should be replaced with v3."},
 {obj:"4.4",c:"ops",q:"NetFlow",a:"#Metadata about network conversations\nWho talked to whom, when, ports and bytes — no packet contents",s:"Flow records of who talked to whom, not payloads",
  x:"NetFlow and similar formats (sFlow, IPFIX) export summaries of network flows from routers and switches: source and destination addresses, ports, protocol, timestamps and byte counts, but not the payload. Analysts use it to spot data exfiltration, beaconing and unusual internal traffic patterns at scale. Exam cue: 'identify which internal host sent a large volume of data to an external IP' without full capture points to NetFlow."},
 {obj:"4.4",c:"ops",q:"Data loss prevention (DLP)",a:"#Stops sensitive data leaving\nInspects content in email, web, USB and cloud; blocks or encrypts policy matches",s:"Blocks sensitive data from leaving via email, USB, web",
  x:"DLP systems classify and inspect data in motion, at rest and in use, matching patterns such as credit card numbers or classification labels, and then block, quarantine, encrypt or alert when policy is violated. Endpoint DLP controls USB and printing; network and cloud DLP cover email and SaaS. Exam cue: 'prevent employees from emailing customer PII outside the company' or 'block copying files to removable media' signals DLP."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ops",q:"User behavior analytics (UBA/UEBA)",a:"#Baseline normal, flag the abnormal\nLogging in at 3 a.m. from a new country or bulk downloads triggers an alert",s:"Detects deviations from a user's normal pattern",
  x:"User and entity behavior analytics builds a statistical baseline of each user's and device's typical activity, then scores deviations such as unusual hours, locations, data volumes or access to new systems. It excels at catching compromised credentials and insider threats that signature-based tools miss. Exam cue: 'valid credentials but activity is unusual for that user' points to UEBA."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ops",q:"Firewall rule order and implicit deny",a:"#Top-down, first match wins; deny at the end\nAnything not explicitly permitted is dropped by the implicit deny",s:"First matching rule wins; unmatched traffic is dropped",v:"fwrules",
  x:"Firewalls and ACLs evaluate rules sequentially from the top and apply the first rule that matches, so a broad allow placed above a specific deny renders the deny useless. At the bottom sits an implicit deny that drops everything no rule permitted, which is why allowed services must be listed explicitly. Exam cue: 'traffic is still allowed despite a deny rule' means check rule order; 'why is the new service blocked' is the implicit deny."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ops",q:"Web filter with URL scanning and reputation",a:"#Block sites by category, content or reputation\nAgent or proxy checks each URL against threat reputation and policy",s:"Blocks URLs by category and reputation score",
  x:"A web filter, whether a proxy, cloud service or endpoint agent, evaluates each requested URL against category lists (gambling, malware), a reputation score derived from threat intelligence, and content inspection, then allows, blocks or warns. Centralized proxies also log browsing for investigations. Exam cue: 'prevent users from visiting newly registered malicious domains' points to URL reputation; 'enforce acceptable use categories' points to the web filter."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ops",q:"SPF (email security)",a:"#Lists which servers may send for a domain\nDNS TXT record; receivers check the sending IP against it",s:"DNS record listing authorized sending IPs",g:"email",
  x:"Sender Policy Framework publishes a DNS TXT record naming the mail servers authorized to send email for a domain; receiving servers compare the connecting IP against that list and can reject or flag mismatches. It checks the envelope sender, not the message content. Exam cue: 'attackers are sending mail that appears to come from our domain' with 'authorized mail servers' in the answer signals SPF."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ops",q:"DKIM (email security)",a:"#Signs outgoing mail with a domain key\nReceiver verifies the signature via the public key in DNS — proves no tampering",s:"Cryptographic signature verified via DNS key",g:"email",
  x:"DomainKeys Identified Mail adds a digital signature header to outbound messages using the sending domain's private key; recipients fetch the public key from DNS and verify that the message body and key headers were not altered in transit and truly came from that domain. Exam cue: 'digital signature on email headers' or 'verify message integrity from the sending domain' points to DKIM rather than SPF."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ops",q:"DMARC (email security)",a:"#Policy for SPF/DKIM failures plus reporting\nTells receivers to none/quarantine/reject and sends aggregate reports",s:"Tells receivers what to do when SPF/DKIM fail",g:"email",
  x:"Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting and Conformance builds on SPF and DKIM: a DNS record states what receivers should do with mail that fails alignment (monitor, quarantine or reject) and where to send reports about who is sending mail as the domain. It is the piece that turns SPF and DKIM into enforcement. Exam cue: 'instruct receiving servers to reject spoofed mail' or 'receive reports on spoofing attempts' points to DMARC."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ops",q:"Network access control (NAC)",a:"#Check the device before it joins\nHealth/posture and identity checks decide the VLAN or deny access",s:"Posture check decides whether a device may connect",
  x:"NAC enforces policy at the moment a device connects, typically with 802.1X: it verifies the user or device identity and assesses health such as patch level, antivirus status and disk encryption, then grants full access, a remediation VLAN or nothing. Agents give detailed posture; agentless NAC uses profiling. Exam cue: 'unpatched laptops should be placed on a remediation network' or 'only compliant devices may join the LAN' signals NAC."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ops",q:"EDR as an enterprise monitoring capability",a:"#Continuous endpoint telemetry with response\nRecords process, file and network activity; enables isolation and hunting",s:"Records endpoint activity and enables isolation",
  x:"From the operations perspective, endpoint detection and response provides the telemetry stream and response actions that analysts rely on: it records every process launch, file write and connection, detects behavioral indicators, and lets the SOC isolate a host, kill processes or pull memory remotely. It feeds SIEM and SOAR. Exam cue: 'investigate what a malware sample did on the host' or 'remotely contain a compromised endpoint' points to EDR."},
 {obj:"4.7",c:"ops",q:"Automation use cases in security operations",a:"#Provisioning, ticketing, enforcement, response\nUser onboarding, guardrails, API integrations, escalation, continuous integration",s:"Onboarding, ticketing, guardrails, integrations",
  x:"Common automation and orchestration use cases include provisioning and de-provisioning accounts, creating and escalating tickets, enforcing security guardrails in cloud and CI/CD pipelines, integrating tools through APIs, and running scripted response actions. These reduce manual error and speed reaction. Exam cue: 'new hires wait days for access' or 'analysts repeat the same containment steps' points to an automation use case."},
 {obj:"4.7",c:"ops",q:"Benefits of security automation",a:"#Speed, consistency, scale, less burnout\nEnforces baselines, reacts in seconds, frees staff for analysis",s:"Faster, consistent response that scales and eases workload",
  x:"Automation delivers faster reaction times, consistent enforcement of baselines, the ability to handle volume without adding staff, and relief from repetitive tasks that cause employee burnout and retention problems. It also acts as a workforce multiplier for small teams. Exam cue: any question stressing 'reduce time to respond' or 'ensure the same steps are followed every time' is asking about automation benefits."},
 {obj:"4.7",c:"ops",q:"Automation considerations — single point of failure and technical debt",a:"#Automation can break at scale\nOne failing script halts everything; unmaintained scripts become debt; cost and complexity",s:"Failure of one script, unmaintained code, complexity",
  x:"Automation introduces its own risks: a script or orchestration platform can become a single point of failure that stops all provisioning or response when it breaks; hastily written automation accumulates technical debt that nobody understands or maintains; and there is ongoing cost, complexity and supportability to consider. Exam cue: 'the automation platform went down and no one could be onboarded' or 'scripts written by a departed engineer' names these considerations."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Nmap -sS",a:"#TCP SYN (half-open) scan\nSends SYN, reads the reply, never completes the handshake — fast and quieter",s:"Half-open SYN scan that never completes the handshake",
  x:"The SYN scan sends a SYN to each port and interprets a SYN/ACK as open and a RST as closed, then sends its own RST instead of completing the three-way handshake, so many applications never log the connection. It requires raw socket privileges and is Nmap's default when run as root. Exam cue: 'stealth scan' or 'does not complete the TCP connection' identifies -sS; -sT is the full connect scan."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Nmap -sV and -O",a:"#-sV detects service versions; -O fingerprints the OS\nBanner and probe responses reveal software; TCP/IP quirks reveal the operating system",s:"Service version detection and OS fingerprinting",
  x:"The -sV option probes open ports to identify the application and version behind them, which is what vulnerability lookups need; -O compares TCP/IP stack behavior against a fingerprint database to guess the operating system. Both are noisier than a plain port scan. Exam cue: 'determine which version of the web server is running' is -sV; 'identify whether the host is Windows or Linux' is -O (or -A for aggressive combined detection)."},
 {obj:"4.3",c:"ops",q:"Nmap -p",a:"#Selects the ports to scan\n-p 22,80 or -p 1-1024; -p- scans all 65535; U: prefix for UDP",s:"Specifies which ports or ranges to scan",
  x:"By default Nmap scans the 1,000 most common TCP ports, and -p overrides that with a list or range, -p- covering every port from 1 to 65535. Prefixes such as U:53 target UDP ports when combined with -sU. Exam cue: a scan that 'missed a service on a high port' needed -p-; a question showing 'nmap -p 443 host' is asking what that single-port scan checks."},
 {obj:"4.5",c:"ops",q:"Reading Linux permissions in ls -l",a:"#Owner, group, others — rwx each\n-rwxr-x--- = owner full, group read/execute, others nothing",s:"Three rwx triplets for owner, group, others",
  x:"The first character of an ls -l entry gives the type (- file, d directory, l link), followed by three groups of read, write and execute bits for the owner, the group and everyone else. Numeric chmod maps r=4, w=2, x=1 per triplet, so chmod 750 yields rwxr-x---. Exam cue: 'world-writable file' means the last triplet includes w (such as 777 or 666); 'restrict a script to its owner' is chmod 700."},
 {obj:"4.9",c:"ops",q:"grep for log analysis",a:"#Search text for a pattern\n-i ignores case, -v inverts, -r recurses, -c counts matches",s:"Filters lines matching a pattern",
  x:"grep prints lines in a file or stream that match a pattern, making it the basic tool for pulling failed logins, IP addresses or error strings out of large logs. Useful flags include -i for case-insensitive, -v to show non-matching lines, -r to search directories recursively and -c to count. Exam cue: 'find every line containing a specific IP address in auth.log' or a command like grep -i \"failed\" is asking about grep."},
 {obj:"4.9",c:"ops",q:"netstat and ss",a:"#List sockets and listening ports\nss -tulpn shows TCP/UDP listeners with owning process",s:"Show open connections and listening services",
  x:"netstat and its modern replacement ss display active network connections, listening ports, routing tables and the processes bound to each socket. Analysts use them during hardening to find unnecessary services and during incident response to spot suspicious outbound connections or unknown listeners. Exam cue: 'identify which process is listening on port 4444' or 'confirm the host has connections to an unknown IP' points to netstat/ss."},

 /* ===== ir ===== */
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"IR step 1 — Preparation",a:"#Build the team, tools and plan before anything happens\nPolicies, contact lists, jump kits, training, hardened systems",s:"Team, tools and plan established beforehand",g:"irstep",v:"irlife",
  x:"Preparation is everything done before an incident: forming and training the CSIRT, writing the IR plan and playbooks, staging forensic tools and secure communications, and hardening systems so incidents are less likely. It is where most of the value of an IR program is created. Exam cue: 'establish a call tree', 'acquire forensic workstations' or 'train the response team' happens in the preparation phase."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"IR step 2 — Detection (identification)",a:"#Recognize that an event is an incident\nTriage alerts, confirm indicators, classify and declare",s:"Confirm and declare that an incident is occurring",g:"irstep",v:"irlife",
  x:"Detection, called identification in some models, is where monitoring output, user reports or threat hunting reveal signs of compromise and the team decides whether they constitute a security incident, then classifies its severity and notifies stakeholders. Accurate detection depends on good logging and tuned alerting. Exam cue: 'the SOC receives an alert and verifies the activity is malicious' describes the detection phase."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"IR step 3 — Analysis",a:"#Determine scope, impact and how it happened\nExamine logs and artifacts to find affected systems and the attack path",s:"Determine scope, affected systems and attack path",g:"irstep",v:"irlife",
  x:"Analysis digs into the confirmed incident to determine which systems, accounts and data are affected, what the attacker did, how they got in and what indicators can be used to find further compromise. It drives the containment strategy and continues throughout the response. Exam cue: 'identify which other hosts show the same indicators' or 'establish the timeline of the attack' belongs to analysis."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"IR step 4 — Containment",a:"#Stop the spread without destroying evidence\nIsolate hosts, block IPs, disable accounts; short-term then long-term",s:"Limit spread while preserving evidence",g:"irstep",v:"irlife",
  x:"Containment limits the damage: isolating infected hosts from the network, blocking attacker addresses, disabling compromised accounts and segmenting affected systems, while balancing the need to preserve evidence and keep the business running. Short-term containment stops the bleeding; long-term containment holds until eradication is done. Exam cue: 'prevent the malware from reaching other systems' or 'first action after confirming the compromise' is containment."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"IR step 5 — Eradication",a:"#Remove the cause completely\nDelete malware, close the vulnerability, reset credentials, rebuild if needed",s:"Remove malware and close the entry point",g:"irstep",v:"irlife",
  x:"Eradication removes the threat from the environment: wiping malware, deleting attacker accounts and persistence mechanisms, patching the vulnerability that was exploited and reimaging systems that cannot be trusted. It must be complete before recovery or the attacker simply returns. Exam cue: 'remove the rootkit and patch the exploited service' or 'ensure the attacker's foothold is gone' is eradication."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"IR step 6 — Recovery",a:"#Restore systems to normal operation\nRebuild from clean images or backups, validate, monitor closely",s:"Restore and validate systems back into production",g:"irstep",v:"irlife",
  x:"Recovery brings affected systems back into production from clean backups or fresh builds, verifies they function correctly and are no longer compromised, and applies heightened monitoring for a period to catch any resurgence. Restore order follows business priority. Exam cue: 'restore the database from the last known-good backup and return it to service' describes recovery."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"IR step 7 — Lessons learned",a:"#Post-incident review to improve\nWhat happened, what worked, what to change; update plans and controls",s:"Post-incident review that updates the plan",g:"irstep",v:"irlife",
  x:"The lessons learned (post-incident) phase gathers the team soon after closure to document the timeline, evaluate how well detection and response worked, identify root causes and assign action items that update policies, playbooks, controls and training. It feeds back into preparation. Exam cue: 'meeting after the incident to discuss improvements' or 'update the IR plan based on the event' is lessons learned."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Incident response training",a:"#Prepare people to execute the plan\nRole-specific training so responders and users know what to do and whom to call",s:"Teach staff their roles before an incident",
  x:"IR training ensures that responders know the tools, procedures and escalation paths, and that all employees know how to recognize and report suspected incidents. It is part of preparation and is refreshed after lessons learned or plan changes. Exam cue: 'employees did not know how to report the phishing email' or 'responders were unfamiliar with the playbook' points to a training gap."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Tabletop exercise vs simulation",a:"#Tabletop talks; simulation acts\nDiscussion-based walkthrough versus hands-on drill against a mock incident",s:"Discussion walkthrough vs hands-on drill",
  x:"A tabletop exercise gathers stakeholders to talk through a scenario step by step, testing decision-making, roles and communication without touching production systems; it is cheap and low risk. A simulation (or full-scale/functional exercise) has the team actually perform response actions against a staged incident, testing tools and technical skills. Exam cue: 'discuss how the team would respond' is tabletop; 'practice the actual procedures' is simulation."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Root cause analysis (RCA)",a:"#Find the underlying reason, not the symptom\nAsk why repeatedly until the fixable origin is found",s:"Identifies the underlying cause to prevent recurrence",
  x:"Root cause analysis systematically traces an incident back to the fundamental failure that allowed it, such as a missing patch process or an unreviewed firewall change, rather than stopping at the immediate symptom. Techniques include five whys and fishbone diagrams. Exam cue: 'prevent the same incident from happening again' or 'the same issue keeps recurring' calls for RCA, typically during lessons learned."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Threat hunting",a:"#Proactively search for undetected intrusions\nHypothesis-driven, uses threat intel and telemetry rather than waiting for alerts",s:"Proactive hypothesis-driven search for hidden attackers",
  x:"Threat hunting assumes the adversary may already be inside and proactively looks for evidence using hypotheses built from threat intelligence, MITRE ATT&CK techniques and knowledge of the environment, querying EDR, SIEM and NetFlow data. Findings often become new detection rules. Exam cue: 'search for indicators of compromise that existing tools have not alerted on' points to threat hunting, as opposed to reactive incident response."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Playbooks vs runbooks",a:"#Playbook: steps for a specific incident type\nRunbook: the detailed, often automated procedure for one task within it",s:"Incident-type checklist vs step-level procedure",
  x:"A playbook is a documented checklist for handling a particular incident category, such as ransomware or a phishing campaign, covering decisions, roles and communication. A runbook is a granular, repeatable procedure for a single operation like isolating a host, and is often what SOAR automates. Exam cue: 'standard set of actions for handling a phishing report' is a playbook; 'automated sequence of technical steps' is a runbook."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Legal hold",a:"#Preserve data relevant to litigation\nSuspends normal deletion/retention for the affected records",s:"Suspends deletion of records needed for litigation",
  x:"A legal hold is a directive, usually from counsel, to preserve all documents, emails and data that may be relevant to pending or anticipated litigation or investigation, overriding normal retention schedules and automatic deletion. Failing to honor it can bring sanctions for spoliation. Exam cue: 'lawyers notified IT that a lawsuit is expected' followed by 'what must be done with the mailboxes' is a legal hold."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Chain of custody",a:"#Documented handling of evidence from seizure to court\nWho collected, transferred, stored and accessed it, when and why",s:"Record of who handled evidence and when",
  x:"Chain of custody is the unbroken, signed record showing every person who collected, transported, stored or examined a piece of evidence, with dates, times and reasons, and how it was secured between hand-offs. Any gap allows the defense to argue tampering and get the evidence excluded. Exam cue: 'ensure the evidence is admissible' or 'document who has handled the drive' points to chain of custody."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Order of volatility",a:"#Capture the most short-lived data first\nCPU/cache → RAM/network state → disk → backups/archives",s:"Collect RAM and network state before disk",
  x:"The order of volatility ranks evidence by how quickly it disappears: CPU registers and cache, then memory contents, routing and ARP tables, process lists and network connections, then temporary files, disk, and finally remote logs, backups and archival media. Collect in that sequence so powering down does not destroy the most valuable data. Exam cue: 'which should be collected first' on a live system is RAM (after cache/registers), never the hard drive."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Forensic acquisition and imaging",a:"#Bit-for-bit copy with a write blocker\nWork on the image; keep the original pristine",s:"Bit-level copy made through a write blocker",
  x:"Acquisition creates a complete bit-for-bit image of storage (dd, FTK Imager) through a hardware write blocker so the original is never altered, capturing deleted files and slack space as well as live files. Memory acquisition uses tools that dump RAM before shutdown. Analysts examine the copy, and often a copy of the copy. Exam cue: 'prevent changes to the original drive during collection' means a write blocker; 'capture unallocated space' means a full image."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Hashing evidence for integrity",a:"#Hash before and after to prove no change\nMatching SHA-256 of original and image shows the copy is exact and untampered",s:"Matching hashes prove the image is unaltered",
  x:"Immediately after acquisition the examiner computes a cryptographic hash of the original media and of the image; identical values prove the copy is exact, and re-hashing later proves nothing changed during analysis or storage. The hash is recorded in the chain of custody. Exam cue: 'demonstrate the evidence was not modified' or 'verify the forensic copy' is answered with hashing (SHA-256 or MD5 for comparison)."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Preservation of evidence",a:"#Keep it intact and protected\nSecure storage, environmental controls, access limited and logged",s:"Secure, logged storage that keeps evidence intact",
  x:"Preservation covers everything done to keep evidence in its original state from collection until it is no longer needed: bagging and labeling, anti-static and climate-controlled storage, locked evidence rooms with access logs, and retaining images for the legally required period. It supports the chain of custody and admissibility. Exam cue: 'store the seized drives so they remain admissible' or 'protect evidence from degradation' signals preservation."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"E-discovery",a:"#Producing electronic records for legal proceedings\nIdentify, collect, review and hand over relevant ESI",s:"Locating and producing electronic records for a case",
  x:"Electronic discovery is the legal process of identifying, preserving, collecting, reviewing and producing electronically stored information such as email, documents and chat logs in response to litigation or regulatory requests. It overlaps with forensics but focuses on relevant content rather than attacker artifacts, and legal hold is its starting point. Exam cue: 'provide all emails relating to the contract to opposing counsel' describes e-discovery."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Forensic reporting",a:"#Document methods, findings and conclusions\nRepeatable process, tools and versions, plain-language summary for non-experts",s:"Documents methods and findings so results are repeatable",
  x:"The forensic report records what was examined, the tools and procedures used, the findings with supporting evidence, and conclusions, written so another qualified examiner could repeat the process and a non-technical audience such as a jury can follow it. Accuracy and neutrality matter because the report may be challenged in court. Exam cue: 'present the results of the investigation to management or legal' points to the reporting phase."},
 {obj:"4.9",c:"ir",q:"Firewall and IDS/IPS logs as data sources",a:"#Show blocked/allowed connections and signature hits\nReveal scans, C2 attempts and exploit signatures at the perimeter",s:"Perimeter connection decisions and signature alerts",
  x:"Firewall logs record permitted and denied connections with addresses, ports and rule matched, which exposes port scans, outbound beaconing and policy violations. IDS/IPS logs add signature or anomaly matches describing the suspected attack. Together they establish when an attacker first probed and what got through. Exam cue: 'determine whether the exploit attempt reached the server' or 'find the source IP of the scan' points to these logs."},
 {obj:"4.9",c:"ir",q:"Endpoint, application and OS-specific logs",a:"#Host-level record of what actually ran\nWindows Event/Security logs, syslog, app error logs, EDR telemetry",s:"Host logs of logins, processes and app events",
  x:"Operating system logs (Windows Security and System logs, Linux auth.log and syslog) show logons, privilege use, service starts and crashes; application logs show authentication failures, errors and transactions inside a specific program; endpoint security logs add process trees and detections. Exam cue: 'which account logged in to the server at that time' is the OS security log; 'errors before the web app crashed' is the application log."},
 {obj:"4.9",c:"ir",q:"Metadata, packet captures and vulnerability scan output",a:"#Context, contents and known weaknesses\nMetadata dates and authors; pcap holds full payloads; scans show what was exploitable",s:"File metadata, full packet payloads, known flaws",
  x:"Metadata such as file timestamps, author fields, email headers and EXIF data establishes when and by whom artifacts were created. Packet captures preserve the entire content of network conversations, enabling reconstruction of exfiltrated files or exploit payloads. Vulnerability scan reports tell investigators which weaknesses existed on the victim at the time. Exam cue: 'reconstruct the exact data sent' is pcap; 'prove when the document was modified' is metadata."},
 {obj:"4.9",c:"ir",q:"Dashboards and network logs in investigations",a:"#Summarize and correlate traffic patterns\nSIEM dashboards visualize trends; switch/router/NetFlow logs trace internal movement",s:"Visual trend summaries and internal traffic records",
  x:"Dashboards present aggregated log data as charts and counters so responders can spot spikes in failed logins, blocked traffic or data volume at a glance and drill into detail. Network device logs and flow records trace lateral movement between internal hosts that perimeter firewalls never see. Exam cue: 'quickly show management the scope of the attack' is a dashboard; 'which internal systems the compromised host connected to' is network/flow logs."},
 {obj:"4.8",c:"ir",q:"Cyber Kill Chain — the sequence",a:"#Recon → weaponize → deliver → exploit → install → C2 → actions\nBreak any link early to stop the objective",s:"Seven stages from reconnaissance to actions on objectives",v:"killchain",
  x:"Lockheed Martin's Cyber Kill Chain models an intrusion as seven steps: reconnaissance, weaponization, delivery, exploitation, installation, command and control, and actions on objectives. Defenders map controls to each stage so an attack can be disrupted before it reaches its goal, and analysts use it to describe how far an intrusion progressed. Exam cue: 'at which stage did the malware establish a channel back to the attacker' is command and control; MITRE ATT&CK is the more granular alternative."},

 /* ===== gov ===== */
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Acceptable use policy (AUP)",a:"#Rules for using company systems\nWhat employees may and may not do with equipment, network and internet",s:"Rules for employee use of company systems",g:"policy",
  x:"The acceptable use policy defines how employees may use organizational assets such as computers, email, internet and mobile devices, and what is prohibited, including personal use limits, illegal content and monitoring consent. Users typically sign it at onboarding, giving the organization grounds for discipline. Exam cue: 'employee streaming movies on the corporate network' or 'document users sign about appropriate use' points to the AUP."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Information security policy",a:"#Top-level statement of security intent\nManagement's commitment, scope, roles and the framework other policies hang from",s:"Overarching statement of management's security goals",g:"policy",
  x:"The information security policy is the umbrella document, endorsed by senior management, that states the organization's security objectives, scope, responsibilities and the principles from which specific policies, standards and procedures derive. It signals executive commitment and provides authority for enforcement. Exam cue: 'high-level document approved by executives that establishes the security program' is the information security policy."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Business continuity (BC) policy",a:"#Keep critical operations running during disruption\nDefines priorities, alternate processes and responsibilities to sustain the business",s:"Sustain critical operations through a disruption",g:"policy",
  x:"The business continuity policy establishes how the organization will maintain essential functions during and after a disruptive event, covering priorities from the BIA, alternate work arrangements, supplier dependencies and who declares an event. It is broader than disaster recovery, which focuses on restoring IT. Exam cue: 'continue serving customers while the main office is unavailable' is business continuity."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Disaster recovery (DR) policy",a:"#Restore IT systems after a disaster\nRecovery sites, backup strategy, RTO/RPO targets and restoration order",s:"Restore IT systems and data after a disaster",g:"policy",
  x:"The disaster recovery policy governs how technology systems and data are recovered after a major outage: which recovery site model is used, backup and replication requirements, recovery time and recovery point objectives, and the order in which systems are restored. It is the IT-focused subset of business continuity. Exam cue: 'restore the data center at a hot site within four hours' is disaster recovery."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Incident response policy",a:"#Authority and framework for handling incidents\nDefines what an incident is, who responds, escalation and reporting duties",s:"Defines incident authority, roles and escalation",g:"policy",
  x:"The incident response policy gives the CSIRT its mandate: it defines what constitutes an incident, assigns roles and authority to act (such as disconnecting systems), sets escalation and notification requirements including legal and regulatory reporting, and references the detailed IR plan and playbooks. Exam cue: 'document that authorizes the team to take systems offline during an attack' is the IR policy."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Software development lifecycle (SDLC) policy",a:"#Security built into every development phase\nRequirements, secure coding, testing, code review and release gates",s:"Security requirements at each development phase",g:"policy",
  x:"An SDLC policy mandates security activities throughout development: threat modeling and requirements at design, secure coding standards, static and dynamic testing, peer review, and approval gates before release, whether waterfall or agile. It prevents security from being bolted on at the end. Exam cue: 'developers must complete code review and SAST before merging' is an SDLC policy requirement."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Change management policy",a:"#Controlled, approved, documented changes\nRequest, impact analysis, approval, test, backout plan, update documentation",s:"Formal approval and documentation of changes",g:"policy",
  x:"The change management policy requires that modifications to systems, configurations and code go through a formal process: submitting a request, assessing impact and risk, obtaining approval from a change advisory board, testing, scheduling in a maintenance window with a backout plan, and updating documentation. It prevents unplanned outages and unauthorized changes. Exam cue: 'an admin altered the firewall without approval and caused an outage' is a change management failure."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Standards — password, access control, physical, encryption",a:"#Mandatory specifics that implement policy\nMinimum length, MFA requirements, badge rules, AES-256 and TLS 1.2+",s:"Mandatory technical specifics that support policy",
  x:"Standards translate policy intent into required, measurable specifics: the password standard sets length, history and MFA rules; the access control standard defines how rights are requested and reviewed; the physical security standard covers badges, visitor escorts and server room locks; the encryption standard names approved algorithms, key lengths and protocols. Exam cue: 'document that specifies AES-256 must be used' is a standard, not a policy or guideline."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Procedures — onboarding, offboarding and playbooks",a:"#Step-by-step instructions\nExactly how to provision a new hire, revoke a leaver, or respond to ransomware",s:"Step-by-step instructions for a specific task",
  x:"Procedures are detailed, ordered steps for carrying out a task consistently: onboarding covers background checks, NDA, account creation and training; offboarding covers access revocation, asset return and exit interview; playbooks give the sequence for handling a specific incident type. They sit below policies and standards in the document hierarchy. Exam cue: 'checklist an administrator follows when an employee leaves' is an offboarding procedure."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Guidelines — how they differ from standards",a:"#Recommended, not mandatory\nBest-practice advice that allows judgment where standards don't apply",s:"Recommended best practice, not mandatory",
  x:"Guidelines offer recommended practices and advice, such as tips for choosing strong passphrases or securing a home office, that help people meet policy but permit flexibility. Standards and policies are mandatory and enforceable; guidelines are not. Exam cue: the words 'recommended', 'suggested' or 'best practice with flexibility' identify a guideline, while 'must' or 'required' identify a policy or standard."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"External considerations — regulatory, legal, industry, local/global",a:"#Outside requirements shape internal policy\nLaws, regulators, industry rules like PCI DSS, and differing national regimes",s:"Laws, regulators, industry rules and jurisdictions",
  x:"Governance must account for external drivers: regulations such as HIPAA or GDPR enforced by government bodies, general legal obligations such as breach notification, industry frameworks such as PCI DSS imposed by contract, and the fact that a global organization faces different, sometimes conflicting, requirements in each jurisdiction. Exam cue: 'the company expanded into the EU and must update its policies' points to global/regulatory considerations."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Governance structures — boards, committees, government entities",a:"#Who sets direction and oversight\nBoard sets strategy; committees oversee specifics; regulators impose external rules",s:"Board strategy, committee oversight, regulator rules",
  x:"Governance structures define who decides and who oversees: the board of directors holds ultimate accountability and sets risk appetite, steering or security committees oversee programs and approve policy, and government entities such as regulators and legislatures impose requirements from outside. Exam cue: 'senior leadership body responsible for approving the security strategy' is the board; 'group of managers reviewing policy exceptions' is a committee."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Centralized vs decentralized governance",a:"#One authority vs distributed decision-making\nCentral = consistent but slow; decentralized = agile but inconsistent",s:"Single authority vs business-unit autonomy",
  x:"Centralized governance puts security decisions, policy and control with one corporate function, producing consistency and clear accountability at the cost of speed and local fit. Decentralized governance lets business units or regions set their own controls within a framework, which is responsive but risks gaps and duplicated effort. Exam cue: 'each division runs its own security program' is decentralized; 'single global policy enforced by headquarters' is centralized."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Data owner",a:"#Accountable executive for a data set\nDecides classification and who may access it",s:"Sets classification and approves access",g:"datarole",
  x:"The data owner is the senior person, usually a business executive, who is ultimately accountable for a category of data: setting its classification, deciding who can access it and ensuring appropriate controls exist. Ownership is about accountability, not day-to-day handling. Exam cue: 'who determines the classification level of the customer database' or 'ultimately responsible for the data' is the owner."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Data controller",a:"#Decides why and how personal data is processed\nThe organization that determines purpose and means (GDPR term)",s:"Determines purpose and means of processing",g:"datarole",
  x:"Under privacy law such as GDPR, the data controller is the organization that determines the purposes and means of processing personal data and carries primary legal responsibility for compliance, including lawful basis, subject rights and breach notification. A company collecting customer data is the controller even if a vendor stores it. Exam cue: 'determines what personal data is collected and why' is the controller."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Data processor",a:"#Handles personal data on the controller's instructions\nCloud provider or payroll vendor processing data for a client",s:"Processes data on behalf of the controller",g:"datarole",
  x:"A data processor is a third party that processes personal data only on behalf of and under the instructions of a data controller, such as a cloud provider, payroll service or marketing platform. Processors must follow the controller's terms and secure the data but do not decide why it is collected. Exam cue: 'a SaaS provider storing customer data for a retailer' is the processor; the retailer is the controller."},
 {obj:"5.1",c:"gov",q:"Data custodian / steward",a:"#Implements and maintains the controls\nIT custodian manages storage and backups; steward ensures data quality and labeling",s:"Day-to-day handler implementing owner's controls",g:"datarole",
  x:"The custodian is the technical role, usually in IT, that implements the owner's decisions: configuring access permissions, running backups, applying encryption and monitoring. The steward focuses on the data itself, ensuring it is accurate, consistently labeled and used in line with policy. Exam cue: 'responsible for backing up and securing the file server' is the custodian; 'ensures data is correctly classified and of good quality' is the steward."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Risk identification",a:"#Find the threats and vulnerabilities that matter\nFirst step of the risk process — asset, threat, vulnerability pairs",s:"Cataloging threats and vulnerabilities to assets",
  x:"Risk identification is the first step of risk management: enumerating assets, the threats that could affect them and the vulnerabilities those threats could exploit, using asset inventories, threat intelligence, past incidents, audits and brainstorming. It produces the raw list that assessment will analyze and the register will track. Exam cue: 'compile a list of potential events that could harm the organization' is identification, before analysis or treatment."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Risk assessment types — ad hoc, recurring, one-time, continuous",a:"#When and how often risk is evaluated\nAd hoc on demand; recurring on schedule; one-time per project; continuous via monitoring",s:"On-demand, scheduled, per-project, or ongoing",
  x:"An ad hoc assessment is performed in response to a specific event or question; a recurring assessment runs on a fixed cadence such as annually for compliance; a one-time assessment supports a single project or acquisition; continuous assessment uses automated monitoring and metrics to keep the risk picture current. Exam cue: 'evaluate risk after a major new threat is announced' is ad hoc; 'annual review required by the regulator' is recurring."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Qualitative vs quantitative risk analysis",a:"#Ratings vs dollar figures\nQualitative uses high/medium/low; quantitative calculates SLE and ALE in currency",s:"High/medium/low scale vs monetary calculation",
  x:"Qualitative analysis rates likelihood and impact on descriptive scales and plots them on a heat map, which is fast and works when data is scarce but is subjective. Quantitative analysis assigns monetary values through asset value, exposure factor, SLE, ARO and ALE, supporting cost-benefit decisions but requiring reliable numbers. Exam cue: 'compare the cost of the control to the expected loss' is quantitative; 'ranked as high, medium or low' is qualitative."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Single loss expectancy (SLE)",a:"#Asset value × exposure factor\nCost of one occurrence of the event",s:"Loss from a single occurrence: AV × EF",g:"riskmath",v:"risk",
  x:"Single loss expectancy is the monetary loss expected from one instance of a risk event, calculated as asset value multiplied by exposure factor. A 200,000 server with a 25 percent EF has an SLE of 50,000. It is the building block for ALE. Exam cue: 'how much would one fire/breach/outage cost' is SLE; watch for questions that give AV and EF and ask for the per-event loss."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Annualized rate of occurrence (ARO)",a:"#How many times per year\nExpected frequency — once every 10 years is 0.1",s:"Expected number of occurrences per year",g:"riskmath",v:"risk",
  x:"Annualized rate of occurrence is the estimated number of times a risk event will happen in a year, derived from history, industry data or expert judgment. An event expected every five years has an ARO of 0.2; one expected twice a year has an ARO of 2. It converts per-event loss into annual loss. Exam cue: 'the flood occurs once every 20 years' is asking you to compute ARO = 0.05."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Annualized loss expectancy (ALE)",a:"#SLE × ARO\nExpected loss per year — compare against annual control cost",s:"Expected yearly loss: SLE × ARO",g:"riskmath",v:"risk",
  x:"Annualized loss expectancy multiplies the single loss expectancy by the annualized rate of occurrence to give the expected cost per year from a risk, which is then weighed against the annual cost of a control. If ALE is 10,000 and the control costs 15,000 per year, the control is not cost-justified. Exam cue: any question asking 'expected annual loss' or 'is the control worth the money' is ALE math."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Exposure factor (EF) in risk math",a:"#Percentage of asset value lost in one event\n0–100% — the damage share, not the probability",s:"Percent of asset value destroyed per event",g:"riskmath",v:"risk",
  x:"Exposure factor is the fraction of an asset's value that a specific threat would destroy in a single occurrence, expressed as a percentage, and it is multiplied by asset value to obtain SLE. A fire that would ruin half a warehouse has an EF of 50 percent. It measures severity, not how often the event happens. Exam cue: 'the attack would damage 30 percent of the system's value' is giving you EF."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Probability, likelihood and impact",a:"#Likelihood = chance it happens; impact = how bad\nRisk is the combination of the two — plotted on a heat map",s:"Chance of occurrence combined with severity of harm",
  x:"Likelihood (often called probability in quantitative work) is the estimated chance that a threat will exploit a vulnerability in a given period; impact is the magnitude of harm if it does, in money, downtime, reputation or safety. Risk level is a function of both, which is why a rare but catastrophic event can outrank a frequent minor one. Exam cue: a matrix with likelihood on one axis and impact on the other is a qualitative risk assessment."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Risk register",a:"#Living log of identified risks\nDescription, owner, likelihood, impact, treatment, status and KRIs",s:"Document tracking each risk, its owner and treatment",
  x:"A risk register is the central document or tool listing every identified risk with its description, likelihood and impact rating, assigned risk owner, chosen treatment, current status and key risk indicators used to monitor it. It is reviewed regularly by management and committees. Exam cue: 'where should the newly identified risk and its mitigation plan be recorded' is the risk register."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Key risk indicators (KRIs), risk owner and threshold",a:"#Metrics that warn a risk is rising\nOwner is accountable for the response; threshold is the trigger level for action",s:"Warning metrics with an owner and trigger level",
  x:"Key risk indicators are measurable signals, such as unpatched critical systems or failed login rates, that show a risk moving toward its threshold, the predefined level at which action or escalation is required. Each risk has an owner accountable for monitoring the KRIs and executing the treatment. Exam cue: 'metric that alerts management before the risk materializes' is a KRI; 'person responsible for deciding how the risk is handled' is the risk owner."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Risk appetite vs risk tolerance",a:"#Appetite is the target; tolerance is the acceptable deviation\nExpansionary accepts more, conservative less, neutral balances",s:"Desired risk level vs acceptable variation around it",
  x:"Risk appetite is the amount and type of risk an organization is willing to pursue to meet its objectives, set by the board; risk tolerance is the acceptable variance around that appetite for a specific risk or activity. An expansionary appetite accepts higher risk for growth, a conservative appetite favors caution, and a neutral appetite balances the two. Exam cue: 'the board declared it will accept significant risk to enter new markets' describes an expansionary appetite."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Risk transfer",a:"#Shift the financial impact to another party\nCyber insurance or outsourcing with contractual liability",s:"Insurance or contract shifts the cost to others",g:"riskstrat",
  x:"Risk transference (sharing) moves the financial consequences of a risk to a third party, most commonly by buying cyber insurance or by contractually assigning responsibility to a service provider. The risk itself still exists and reputational damage usually cannot be transferred. Exam cue: 'purchase an insurance policy to cover breach costs' or 'the contract makes the vendor liable' signals transfer."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Risk acceptance",a:"#Acknowledge and live with it\nCost of control exceeds the loss — document the decision and monitor",s:"Formally decide to take no further action",g:"riskstrat",
  x:"Risk acceptance is the documented decision to take no further action against a risk because its expected loss is lower than the cost of mitigation or because it falls within tolerance; the residual risk is monitored and revisited. Formal acceptance is captured via an exemption or exception process. Exam cue: 'the control would cost more than the ALE' or 'management signed off on the residual risk' is acceptance."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Risk avoidance",a:"#Stop doing the risky activity\nDiscontinue the service or don't launch the project at all",s:"Eliminate the activity that creates the risk",g:"riskstrat",
  x:"Risk avoidance removes the risk entirely by not engaging in the activity that creates it, such as declining to store payment card data, shutting down a legacy service or canceling a project. It is the only strategy that reduces the risk to zero, at the cost of the activity's benefits. Exam cue: 'decided not to offer the feature because of the risk' or 'decommission the vulnerable system rather than fix it' is avoidance."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Risk mitigation",a:"#Reduce likelihood or impact with controls\nPatch, encrypt, segment, train — the most common strategy",s:"Apply controls to reduce likelihood or impact",g:"riskstrat",
  x:"Risk mitigation (reduction) implements security controls that lower the likelihood of a threat succeeding or the impact if it does, such as patching, MFA, backups, segmentation and training. It leaves residual risk that must then be accepted or transferred. Exam cue: whenever the answer is 'deploy a control to address the risk', the strategy is mitigation; it is the default choice on most exam scenarios."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Exemption vs exception (risk)",a:"#Exemption: excused from the rule; exception: temporary approved deviation\nBoth documented with compensating controls and an owner",s:"Permanently excused vs temporarily approved deviation",
  x:"An exemption formally excuses a system or process from a policy requirement, often because the rule genuinely does not apply or cannot apply, while an exception grants a time-limited, approved deviation from policy with an expiry date and usually compensating controls. Both must be recorded, risk-accepted by an owner and reviewed. Exam cue: 'legacy system cannot support MFA and management approved running without it for six months' is an exception."},
 {obj:"5.2",c:"gov",q:"Business impact analysis (BIA)",a:"#Identify critical functions and the cost of downtime\nProduces RTO, RPO, MTD and recovery priorities",s:"Determines critical functions and downtime tolerances",
  x:"The business impact analysis identifies mission-essential functions, the systems and dependencies they rely on, and the financial and operational impact of their loss over time, yielding metrics such as maximum tolerable downtime, recovery time objective and recovery point objective. It drives continuity and DR priorities. Exam cue: 'determine which systems must be restored first' or 'how long can the payroll system be down' is answered by the BIA."},
 {obj:"5.4",c:"gov",q:"Compliance reporting — internal vs external",a:"#Proving adherence to obligations\nInternal reports to management; external to regulators, auditors, customers",s:"Reports proving adherence to management or regulators",
  x:"Compliance reporting demonstrates that the organization meets its legal, regulatory and contractual obligations. Internal reporting keeps leadership and committees informed of control status and gaps; external reporting delivers required attestations, filings and evidence to regulators, auditors, partners and customers. Exam cue: 'submit evidence to the regulator annually' is external compliance reporting; 'brief the board on control status' is internal."},
 {obj:"5.4",c:"gov",q:"Consequences of non-compliance",a:"#Fines, sanctions, reputation, license loss, contract breach\nGDPR fines up to 4% of revenue; PCI can revoke card processing",s:"Fines, sanctions, reputational harm, lost licenses",
  x:"Failing to comply can bring monetary fines from regulators, sanctions such as mandated audits or operating restrictions, reputational damage that drives customers away, loss of the license or certification needed to operate (such as the ability to process payment cards), and contractual penalties or termination by partners. Exam cue: 'what could happen if the company ignores the regulation' expects this list; 'lost the ability to accept credit cards' is a PCI DSS consequence."},
 {obj:"5.4",c:"gov",q:"Due diligence vs due care",a:"#Diligence investigates; care acts\nResearch and assess before deciding, then take reasonable ongoing precautions",s:"Investigate before acting vs act reasonably after",
  x:"Due diligence is the investigative work of identifying and understanding risks, such as vetting a vendor or researching legal obligations, before making a decision. Due care is taking the reasonable, prudent actions a responsible organization would take to protect assets, such as implementing and maintaining controls. Together they defend against negligence claims. Exam cue: 'reviewed the vendor's audit reports before signing' is diligence; 'kept systems patched' is due care."},
 {obj:"5.4",c:"gov",q:"Data subject and the right to be forgotten",a:"#The person the data is about can demand erasure\nGDPR grants access, correction, portability and deletion rights",s:"Individual can demand their personal data be deleted",
  x:"The data subject is the identifiable individual whose personal data is being processed. Privacy laws such as GDPR give subjects rights including access to their data, correction, portability and erasure, commonly called the right to be forgotten, which requires the controller to delete data absent an overriding legal reason. Exam cue: 'customer requests that all their records be removed' is the right to be forgotten; the customer is the data subject."},
 {obj:"5.4",c:"gov",q:"Data inventory and retention (privacy)",a:"#Know what personal data you hold and for how long\nMap collection, storage and flows; delete when the purpose ends",s:"Map personal data holdings and set deletion timelines",
  x:"A data inventory (or data map) records what personal data the organization collects, where it is stored, who can access it, why it was collected and with whom it is shared; retention rules then keep it only as long as the stated purpose or law requires. Without an inventory, subject requests and breach notifications cannot be fulfilled accurately. Exam cue: 'respond to a subject access request' or 'minimize personal data kept' depends on the inventory and retention schedule."},
 {obj:"5.4",c:"gov",q:"Local vs global privacy law implications",a:"#Different jurisdictions, different rules\nData sovereignty may require data to stay in-country; strictest law often wins",s:"Jurisdiction dictates where data may live and how",
  x:"Privacy obligations vary by location: a national or state law such as GDPR or CCPA applies based on where the data subjects live, not just where the company sits, and data sovereignty rules can require that citizens' data be stored and processed within borders. Global organizations typically adopt the strictest applicable standard or segment data by region. Exam cue: 'EU customer data must remain in EU data centers' or 'transferring records across borders' raises local versus global privacy implications."},

 /* ===== third ===== */
 {obj:"5.3",c:"third",q:"Vendor assessment methods",a:"#Pen test, right-to-audit, audit evidence, independent assessments\nVerify the vendor's controls before and during the relationship",s:"Pen tests, audit rights and independent reports",
  x:"Assessing a vendor means gathering evidence about their security rather than trusting claims: commissioning or reviewing penetration tests of their systems, negotiating a right-to-audit clause so you can inspect them, obtaining evidence of their internal audits, and reviewing independent third-party assessments such as SOC 2 Type II or ISO 27001 certification. Exam cue: 'confirm the cloud provider's controls without visiting them' points to independent assessment reports."},
 {obj:"5.3",c:"third",q:"Supply chain analysis",a:"#Assess the vendor's vendors too\nHardware, software and service dependencies upstream can introduce risk",s:"Evaluates risk from upstream suppliers and components",
  x:"Supply chain analysis examines every party that contributes to a product or service, including the vendor's own suppliers, software components, manufacturing and logistics, because a compromise anywhere upstream (SolarWinds, counterfeit hardware) reaches the customer. It uses SBOMs, provenance checks and supplier questionnaires. Exam cue: 'malicious update delivered through a trusted vendor' or 'where were the components sourced' is supply chain risk."},
 {obj:"5.3",c:"third",q:"Third-party due diligence and conflict of interest",a:"#Investigate the vendor and its independence\nFinancial health, reputation, ownership, and any ties that bias their judgment",s:"Vet the vendor and check for biased relationships",
  x:"Due diligence on a third party covers financial stability, security posture, legal history, reputation and ownership before signing. A conflict of interest arises when a vendor, auditor or employee has a competing relationship, such as an assessor who also sells the product being evaluated, or a procurement manager with a stake in the supplier, which undermines objectivity. Exam cue: 'the auditor is a partner of the company being audited' is a conflict of interest."},
 {obj:"5.3",c:"third",q:"SLA (service level agreement)",a:"#Measurable performance targets with penalties\n99.9% uptime, four-hour response, credits when missed",s:"Defines measurable service targets and penalties",g:"agreement",
  x:"A service level agreement specifies the measurable levels of service a provider commits to, such as availability percentage, response and resolution times and throughput, along with how they are measured and the remedies or credits if they are missed. It is usually an annex to a broader contract. Exam cue: 'guarantee the provider restores service within four hours' or 'uptime commitment' points to the SLA."},
 {obj:"5.3",c:"third",q:"MOU vs MOA",a:"#Memorandum of understanding is looser; agreement is firmer\nMOU states shared intent; MOA details each party's commitments",s:"Statement of intent vs defined mutual commitments",g:"agreement",
  x:"A memorandum of understanding records that parties intend to work together and outlines broad goals, and is often not legally binding. A memorandum of agreement goes further, specifying the responsibilities and deliverables each party accepts, and can be enforceable. Both are common between government bodies and non-profits. Exam cue: 'informal document expressing willingness to cooperate' is an MOU; 'documents specific obligations of each party' is an MOA."},
 {obj:"5.3",c:"third",q:"MSA vs SOW",a:"#Master agreement sets the terms; SOW defines one project\nMSA is the umbrella contract; each SOW lists deliverables, timeline and price",s:"Umbrella contract vs per-project deliverables",g:"agreement",
  x:"A master service agreement establishes the general legal terms (liability, payment, confidentiality, dispute resolution) governing an ongoing vendor relationship so they need not be renegotiated. A statement of work is issued under the MSA for each engagement, spelling out scope, deliverables, milestones, acceptance criteria and cost. Exam cue: 'document defining the deliverables of a specific engagement' is the SOW; 'overarching terms for all future work' is the MSA."},
 {obj:"5.3",c:"third",q:"NDA (non-disclosure agreement)",a:"#Legal duty to keep information confidential\nSigned before sharing sensitive data with staff, vendors or partners",s:"Binds parties to keep shared information secret",g:"agreement",
  x:"A non-disclosure agreement is a contract in which one or both parties promise not to reveal confidential information they receive, defining what is covered, permitted uses and duration. It is signed by employees at onboarding and by vendors before due diligence or projects begin. Exam cue: 'before sharing the design documents with the contractor' or 'protect trade secrets discussed during negotiations' points to an NDA."},
 {obj:"5.3",c:"third",q:"BPA (business partnership agreement)",a:"#Terms of a joint business venture\nOwnership shares, profit split, decision rights and responsibilities of each partner",s:"Defines a joint venture's shares and responsibilities",g:"agreement",
  x:"A business partnership agreement governs a venture between organizations that share investment, risk and reward, spelling out each partner's contributions, ownership percentages, division of profits and losses, decision-making authority and exit terms. It is broader than a service contract because both parties are principals. Exam cue: 'two companies forming a joint venture need to define how profits are split' is a BPA."},
 {obj:"5.3",c:"third",q:"Vendor monitoring, questionnaires and rules of engagement",a:"#Ongoing oversight after signing\nPeriodic questionnaires and reviews; RoE define what testers may do",s:"Continuing oversight via reviews, surveys and RoE",
  x:"Vendor risk does not end at contract signature: monitoring tracks SLA performance, security incidents and financial changes; questionnaires (often standardized like SIG or CAIQ) collect updated control information periodically; rules of engagement define the scope, timing, methods and contacts for any security testing performed on or with the vendor. Exam cue: 'document that specifies what the penetration testers are allowed to attack and when' is rules of engagement."},
 {obj:"5.5",c:"third",q:"Audit types — internal, external, self-assessment, regulatory examination",a:"#Who performs the review and why\nInternal staff, independent third party, own checklist, or a regulator's inspection",s:"Own staff, outside firm, self-checklist, or regulator",
  x:"Internal audits are performed by the organization's own audit function to check compliance and effectiveness for management. External audits are conducted by an independent third party, producing attestation reports such as SOC 2 that customers can rely on. A self-assessment is a questionnaire or checklist completed by the team itself. A regulatory examination is an inspection by a government or industry regulator. Exam cue: 'independent opinion customers can trust' is external; 'the regulator visited to verify compliance' is an examination."},
 {obj:"5.5",c:"third",q:"Attestation (audit)",a:"#Auditor's formal statement that controls meet criteria\nSOC 2 report is the classic attestation of a provider's controls",s:"Independent formal statement that controls are adequate",
  x:"In the audit context, attestation is a formal, signed assertion by an independent auditor that an organization's controls are designed and operating as described against an agreed standard, giving customers assurance without auditing the provider themselves. SOC 2 and ISO 27001 certificates are examples. Internal attestation, where management certifies compliance in writing, also exists. Exam cue: 'provide customers with assurance from an independent firm' is attestation."},
 {obj:"5.5",c:"third",q:"Penetration test types — physical, offensive, defensive, integrated",a:"#What is being tested and from which side\nPhysical tests entry; offensive is red team; defensive is blue team; integrated is purple",s:"Physical entry, red, blue, or combined purple team",
  x:"A physical penetration test attempts to bypass locks, badges and guards to reach systems. Offensive testing is red-team simulation of an attacker; defensive testing evaluates the blue team's ability to detect and respond; integrated (purple team) testing has attackers and defenders work together to improve detection in real time. Exam cue: 'tailgate into the data center' is physical; 'assess how quickly the SOC detects the attack' is defensive."},
 {obj:"5.5",c:"third",q:"Known environment pen test",a:"#Full knowledge (white box)\nTesters receive architecture, source code and credentials up front",s:"Tester given full documentation and access",g:"pentestenv",
  x:"In a known environment test the testers are given complete information about the target: network diagrams, configurations, source code and accounts, which makes the test thorough and efficient at finding flaws but less realistic as a simulation of an outsider. Older terms are white box or full knowledge. Exam cue: 'testers were given the source code and admin credentials' or 'most comprehensive coverage in limited time' is known environment."},
 {obj:"5.5",c:"third",q:"Partially known environment pen test",a:"#Some knowledge (gray box)\nTester has limited insider info, like a user account or basic network layout",s:"Tester has limited insider information",g:"pentestenv",
  x:"A partially known environment test gives the testers some but not all information, such as a standard user account, IP ranges or high-level architecture, simulating a malicious insider or an attacker who has already gained a foothold. It balances realism and efficiency. Formerly called gray box. Exam cue: 'testers were given a regular employee login but no other details' is partially known."},
 {obj:"5.5",c:"third",q:"Unknown environment pen test",a:"#No prior knowledge (black box)\nTester starts like an external attacker, doing their own reconnaissance",s:"Tester starts with no information like an outsider",g:"pentestenv",
  x:"In an unknown environment test the testers receive no information beyond the company name or a target scope and must perform their own reconnaissance, closely simulating a real external attacker. It is the most realistic but slowest and may miss flaws a known-environment test would find. Formerly black box. Exam cue: 'testers were told only the company name' or 'simulate an external attacker with no insider knowledge' is unknown environment."},
 {obj:"5.5",c:"third",q:"Passive vs active reconnaissance",a:"#Passive never touches the target; active does\nOSINT, DNS records and job postings versus scanning and probing",s:"Observe from public sources vs directly probe target",
  x:"Passive reconnaissance gathers information without sending traffic to the target, using public sources such as WHOIS, DNS records, social media, job postings and search engines, so it cannot be detected. Active reconnaissance interacts directly with the target through port scans, banner grabbing and vulnerability probes, which is more revealing but can trigger alerts. Exam cue: 'running Nmap against the target' is active; 'reviewing the company's LinkedIn profiles' is passive."},
 {obj:"5.6",c:"third",q:"Phishing campaigns (awareness)",a:"#Simulated phishing to train and measure\nTrack click and report rates; coach those who fall for it",s:"Simulated phishing that measures click and report rates",
  x:"Security awareness programs send controlled simulated phishing emails to staff, measure who clicks, submits credentials or reports the message, and deliver just-in-time training to those who fall for it. Over time the metrics show whether awareness is improving. Exam cue: 'measure the effectiveness of security awareness training' or 'reduce the number of employees who click suspicious links' points to a phishing campaign."},
 {obj:"5.6",c:"third",q:"Anomalous behavior recognition and insider threat",a:"#Teach staff to notice risky, unexpected or unintentional behavior\nColleague downloading bulk data, working odd hours, bypassing controls",s:"Staff trained to spot and report unusual coworker behavior",
  x:"Awareness training asks employees to recognize and report anomalous behavior: risky actions like disabling security tools, unexpected activity such as accessing systems unrelated to one's role, and unintentional errors like emailing files to the wrong recipient. These are often the earliest signs of an insider threat, whether malicious or negligent. Exam cue: 'employee noticed a coworker copying customer lists before resigning' is insider threat recognition."},
 {obj:"5.6",c:"third",q:"Operational security (OPSEC) and remote/hybrid work guidance",a:"#Don't leak what attackers can use; secure the home office\nAvoid oversharing, shoulder surfing, public Wi-Fi; use VPN, locks and screen privacy",s:"Limit information leakage and secure remote setups",
  x:"OPSEC training teaches staff to avoid revealing exploitable details such as project names on social media, badge photos, or conversations in public, and to protect screens and documents. Remote and hybrid work guidance extends this to home networks and travel: VPN use, device encryption, separating work and personal use, securing routers and being wary of public Wi-Fi. Exam cue: 'employee posted a photo showing their badge and whiteboard' is an OPSEC failure."},
 {obj:"5.6",c:"third",q:"Awareness training — initial vs recurring, and reporting",a:"#Train at hire, refresh regularly, measure results\nOnboarding baseline, annual and event-driven refreshers, metrics to leadership",s:"Onboarding training plus periodic refreshers and metrics",
  x:"Effective awareness programs deliver initial training during onboarding so every new hire starts with a baseline, then recurring training on a schedule and after major incidents or policy changes to counter forgetting and new threats. Reporting tracks completion rates, phishing results and incident reports so leadership can judge effectiveness and compliance. Exam cue: 'annual training required by the regulator' is recurring; 'employees did not know the policy on day one' points to initial training."},
];
